calamares-settings-raye (0.1~dev7) resolute; urgency=medium

  * The first window after an encrypted install is a Raye window again.
    It was a kdialog --msgbox left in ~/.config/autostart by the
    encryption job, carrying the 48 digits that open the disk in plain
    text, with one OK button: nothing to copy them with, nothing to keep
    them in, and none of the Raye look, since kdialog is a QtWidgets
    program and the Raye look is the QML module org.raye.setup. The job
    only ever showed that box because /usr/bin/raye-first-run was not
    there; it is now, so the branch turns itself off.
  * raye-first-run ships its first page: the recovery key in groups of
    six on the night background, Copy, "Save a copy...", a sentence
    saying what the key is for, and a Done that waits until the person
    says the key is written down. Esc leaves it for the next sign-in.
  * The recovery key notice no longer deletes itself when the box it
    tried to open never ran. kdialog is on its way out of the image, and
    the notice removed its own files whatever kdialog's exit status was,
    so the day kdialog goes the one message about the key would have
    vanished without a word.
  * OWED in debian/test_raye_paths.py goes from 12 to 11:
    etc/xdg/autostart/raye-first-run.desktop is no longer owed.
  * OWED goes from 11 to 10, and the ratchet now measures something it
    could not measure before. debian/raye-paths carried a "?" on
    debian/tmp/usr/bin/raye-first-run while app/firstrun was written and
    installed exactly that binary; the marker was stale, and the test
    could not have noticed, because it asked whether a BUILD OUTPUT
    exists and a build output never exists outside a package build. Both
    debian/tmp entries were therefore owed forever, whatever anybody
    wrote. raye_paths.producing_part() now answers for them -- which part
    of RAYE_PARTS installs the file, read from CMakeLists.txt and not
    copied -- so the day app/setup lands the figure comes down by itself.
  * raye-paths stops reporting a build output it cannot see as a missing
    part. Under RAYE_PATHS_STRICT=1 outside a package build it said
    "debian/tmp/usr/bin/raye-first-run: missing" about a file whose part
    was written: an error about its own working conditions, read as an
    answer about the tree. Such an entry is now reported under its own
    word, NOT JUDGED HERE, on stderr and with the count -- not folded in
    with the notes, where a gate comes to look green over something it
    never looked at. And a build output that really has no part now says
    so: "no part of RAYE_PARTS installs it".

 -- techoraye <190692880+techoraye@users.noreply.github.com>  Thu, 06 Nov 2026 12:00:00 +0000

calamares-settings-raye (0.1~dev6) resolute; urgency=medium

  * Installing next to Windows is no longer refused. The disk page would
    not go on when the EFI partition already on the disk was smaller
    than it asked for, and it asked for 300 MiB: Windows makes its own
    at 100 MB, which is 95 MiB. The message spoke of MiB and said
    nothing about why, so there was no way out of it.
  * The disk is btrfs and nothing else. Three of the four ways in took
    their filesystem from the settings; the fourth, manual
    partitioning, lists every filesystem whose tools are installed and
    has no setting to narrow that list. It is off, and what it was there
    for is covered: free space next to the systems already installed, or
    a partition of one's own, both on btrfs.

